.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt NSHD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nshd
.Nd network configuration daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl bdv
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
holds the system's configuration.
It applies every change and answers every show for
.Xr nsh 8
and
.Xr nshdeck 8 ,
which reach it over two sockets and need no privilege.
It is privilege separated: only one of its processes, system, runs as
root and changes the host.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl b
Boot: make the running configuration the saved one before taking
clients (see
.Sx BOOT ) .
.Xr rc.d 8
starts
.Nm
with this option.
.It Fl d
Do not daemonize, and log to standard error.
.It Fl v
Produce more verbose output.
.El
.Sh PROCESSES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  nsh, nshdeck                     anyone
       |  nshd.sock                  |  nshd.ro.sock
   frontend   one for each client   show   four
       |
    config ---- journal   the shared candidate
       |
    system ---- parser    one for each reading
       |
  kernel, daemons, saved configuration
.Ed
.Pp
A request goes down this picture and gains no authority on the way:
system acts on it by the permissions of the session it came from (see
.Sx SESSIONS
and
.Sx PERMISSIONS ) .
.Pp
Each process except the parent and system runs as a user of its own, in
a
.Xr chroot 2
that is the user's home directory, so no process can signal one of
another role or reach its files.
.Bl -tag -width "frontend"
.It parent
Root.
Binds the sockets, starts the others, and accepts each client of the
read-write socket.
It reads nothing a client sends.
.It show
Four, as _nshshow.
They answer the read-only socket, with what anyone may see.
There are four so that one long answer holds up no other.
A show is not
.Xr pledge 2 Ns d :
no promise allows the interface ioctls it uses.
.It frontend
One for each client of the read-write socket, as _nshfront, each in a
process group of its own.
It parses what its client sends and passes typed values to config.
It holds no candidate.
The frontends share one user, so each is
.Xr pledge 2 Ns d
to signal nothing outside its own process group.
.It config
As _nshconf,
.Xr pledge 2 Ns d
to stdio, recvfd and sendfd.
It holds the sessions and their candidates (see
.Sx CANDIDATES ) ,
and requests from system what they need.
It opens no file, runs no program and parses no words.
.It system
Root.
It reads and changes the kernel, the daemons and the saved
configuration (see
.Sx COMMITS
and
.Sx PROGRAMS ) .
It answers only config, and reads no configuration text.
It runs in a session of its own: the programs it starts can outlive
it, so when
.Nm
ends the parent kills what is left in that session, and every process
of _nshcheck's.
.It journal
As _nshjournal.
It stores the shared candidate for config, so the candidate outlives
.Nm .
.It parser
As _nshparse,
.Xr pledge 2 Ns d
to stdio.
system starts a new one for each reading of configuration text, so a
parser keeps nothing from one reading to the next.
.El
.Pp
Each pair of processes that talk has a pipe of its own, which the
parent makes, so a pipe says who sent a message.
A process has only the descriptors the parent placed for it.
config and journal have no pipe to the parent: when the parent ends,
system does, config finds system's pipe closed, and journal config's.
.Pp
.Nm
starts only if each of these users, and _nshcheck, exists, is not root
and has a uid of its own.
.Sh SOCKETS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /var/run/nshd.sock
The read-write socket, for root and the members of the group nshadmin.
Each client is a session.
.It Pa /var/run/nshd.ro.sock
The read-only socket, for anyone.
It answers interfaces and addresses, routes and route lookups, the ARP
and neighbor tables, VLANs, bridges, the kernel statistics that need no
root, the IPsec flows, the daemons that run, the version, who is logged
in and the hostname.
.El
.Sh SESSIONS
A session is one client of the read-write socket, with its frontend.
It makes one request at a time.
.Pp
system reads who the client is from the client's socket, with
.Xr getpeereid 2 .
Nothing the client sends says who it is.
.Pp
config hears of a session only from system, once system has the
client's socket.
So config has no session that system cannot check.
.Pp
config names the session of each request, and system acts on a request
only while that session's client is still connected.
.Pp
A session ends when its client goes.
system checks each second that each client is still connected, so a
frontend that stays after its client keeps the session for about a
second at most.
A session's number is never used again.
.Pp
What system has begun for a session, such as a commit, runs to its end
after the session ends.
What it has not begun is dropped.
.Pp
The limits are 64 sessions at once, 16 unfinished requests for one
session, and 1024 for all.
A session that asks a 17th is ended.
A request counts until system ends it, even after its session has
gone.
.Sh PERMISSIONS
A session has the permissions of its user's login class (see
.Cm login class
in
.Xr nsh 8 ) .
.Bl -column "configure" -offset indent
.It view Ta a show, a watch, a log
.It operate Ta an operation
.It configure Ta Cm configure private , Cm configure terminal ,
a commit, a rollback, a load, a daemon's rules
.It admin Ta Cm configure
and
.Cm configure exclusive ,
the admin lines, the password hashes, authlog
.It exec Ta the programs nsh runs; nsh checks it
.El
.Pp
config checks every request against the permissions the session has.
system checks a commit, a save, a test, an operation and a log again,
against the class as it is then.
A session without admin is sent no password hash, and its commit may
keep an admin line only as it runs already.
.Pp
A commit or a rollback that takes a permission from a session lowers
that session first.
system decides who loses what, from the login lines before and after.
config lowers each such session, ends a web session whose user may no
longer log in, and tells system, which then runs the change.
Until the change ends,
.Nm
takes no new session and refuses web logins.
If config has not answered within 10 seconds, system acts as it does
when config ends (see
.Sx FAILURE ) .
.Pp
A session that is lowered keeps what system had already begun for it,
which runs to its end.
Everything else follows the new class: a log it may no longer follow
stops, a configuration that reaches it afterwards comes without
password hashes even if it asked before, and a session in
.Cm configure
that lost admin may only leave.
.Pp
nsh opens a session before each program it runs and reads that
session's permissions, so a lost exec or operate counts at the next
program.
A permission a class gains counts in system's checks at once, and in
config's only once the session connects again.
.Sh CANDIDATES
A candidate is a copy of the configuration that a session's lines
change.
It starts from a snapshot of what runs, which system takes as the
session enters configure, and again each time the candidate is shown.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm configure private
A candidate of the session's own.
.It Cm configure
One candidate that its sessions share.
Each sees what the others change, and a commit commits all of it.
journal stores it, and a change to it is answered once it is on disk.
A change that journal cannot store fails, but stays in the candidate,
and is stored with the next one journal can store.
.It Cm configure exclusive
The shared candidate, while no other session may change it or commit.
It is refused while another user has uncommitted changes there.
Leaving it discards what was not committed.
.It Cm configure terminal
A candidate made again from the running configuration for each line.
A line that changes it is committed at once, and not saved.
It is refused while a commit is unconfirmed, whose rollback would take
the line back too.
.El
.Pp
.Ic nsh Fl c
sends a whole configuration, which is committed in place of the running
one, all of it or none.
.Cm rollback Ar N
loads saved configuration
.Ar N
into the candidate.
A session in configure may also set a daemon's rules in its candidate,
up to 1 MB.
.Sh COMMITS
Commits run one at a time, for all sessions.
.Bl -enum
.It
config checks the candidate, and sends system the configuration the
commit is based on, the configuration it wants, and the daemons' rules
it changes.
It sends no script.
.It
system tests the rules.
A commit a test refuses runs nothing.
.It
A parser makes the script that leads from what runs to what is wanted.
system checks every edit of it before it runs any: each names a command
a configuration has, where it belongs, with values of its fields' types
and within their bounds.
A line of a command that is no part of a configuration, such as
.Ic reboot ,
.Ic ping
or
.Ic \&! ,
has no edit, and a commit with one is refused.
.It
system records the commit on disk, and runs the script.
.It
The commit took only if what runs is then what was wanted and no edit
failed.
If so, system saves the running configuration.
If not, system undoes the script, back to what it read as the commit
began.
.El
.Pp
system begins a commit only while its session's client is still
connected.
Once begun, the commit runs to its end even if the session goes.
.Pp
An edit that runs a daemon's program fails unless the program exits 0,
whatever it prints.
A disable's
.Xr pkill 1
may also exit 1, for no such daemon ran.
.Xr pf 4 Ns 's
enable and disable succeed only if pf is then on or off.
.Pp
.Cm commit check
stops after the third step, so a candidate the parser can make no
script for fails the check.
A commit outside configure only saves the running configuration.
A commit is refused if the running configuration is not the one it was
planned from.
.Pp
A commit that neither saves nor is undone leaves its record, and the
next commit reports what it left.
While a record says a commit's changes were not saved, a commit may
only save the running configuration or restore the one from before.
.Pp
A line takes an address in numeric form only:
.Nm
sends no DNS query.
.Sh COMMIT CONFIRMED
.Cm commit confirmed
keeps the running configuration unsaved until its deadline, counted in
minutes from when the commit ends.
A commit that saves confirms it.
Its script runs even if the candidate is what runs already, so the
deadline is set.
.Pp
Its rollback configuration is on disk before anything is applied.
It is what ran before the first unconfirmed commit, not the last: a
second
.Cm commit confirmed
does not move what a rollback goes back to.
.Pp
After the deadline, system rolls back by itself, once no commit or job
runs: a commit's undo could restore what the rollback removed.
It demotes
.Xr carp 4
by 128, and promotes it again once the rollback
configuration runs.
.Pp
A rollback that fails is tried again 5 seconds later, three times in
all.
Then carp stays demoted until a commit saves.
.Pp
When
.Nm
starts, system rolls back an unconfirmed commit whose deadline has
passed.
While
.Nm
does not run, nothing rolls it back.
That holds too for a commit that system kept after config had ended.
.Sh BOOT
With
.Fl b ,
system makes the running configuration the saved one before
.Nm
takes clients.
.Pp
The boot runs once for each boot of the host, which its record names.
An
.Nm
started again runs it again only if it did not finish or failed.
.Pp
A line that fails is reported and the rest still run: the boot undoes
nothing.
.Pp
A rollback that is due as
.Nm
starts runs first.
A boot that succeeds removes an unconfirmed commit's record, since what
runs is then its rollback configuration.
.Pp
What the checks and the daemons' tests find is logged, and the boot
goes on.
.Pp
carp is demoted by 128 until the boot succeeds, or, if it fails, until
a commit saves.
.Pp
.Nm
takes clients after a boot that failed too, so the configuration can be
repaired.
.Pp
The process
.Xr rc 8
started returns 0 if the saved configuration then runs, else 1.
.Pp
The first time, when there is no saved configuration, system saves
.Pa /etc/nshrc .
.Sh PROGRAMS
system runs each program in a child, in a process group of its own,
with the environment
.Xr rc 8
gives.
The program comes from nsh's tables, never from a request.
.Pp
A show's program, and a test of a daemon's rules, a commit's or vmd's
check-config, run as _nshcheck.
The exceptions keep root: a program that uses a daemon's control
socket,
.Pa /dev/pf
or PF_KEY, and the tests of relayd, ldapd, npppd, sasyncd and sshd,
which read keys.
.Pp
A log and dhcpd's leases are opened as root and read as _nshcheck.
.Pp
.Cm show vmd status
asks
.Xr vmd 8
on its control socket from a child that runs as the session's user, so
the socket decides whether that user may ask.
The child sends its answer to system as records, and system passes on
only VMs and messages.
pf's log comes from
.Xr tcpdump 8 ,
which is started as root because it gives up root itself.
A log is followed across its rotation by
.Xr newsyslog 8 .
.Pp
Any other operation, a commit's script and a save run as root.
.Pp
A show's output is sent only as fast as its client reads it, and its
child is killed if the client goes.
.Pp
A job that runs past its limit fails.
Its process group gets
.Dv SIGTERM ,
then
.Dv SIGKILL :
.Bl -column "a script" "900 seconds" -offset indent
.It a script Ta 900 seconds
.It a save Ta 300 seconds
.It a test Ta 120 seconds
.It a parser's reading Ta 60 seconds
.It a web login's check Ta 30 seconds
.El
.Pp
While a script, a save or a test runs, other requests wait, and streams
go on.
.Sh WEB SESSIONS
A client with _nshweb's uid is
.Xr nshdeck 8 .
Its session has no permissions until it logs in, as a user, and then
has that user's.
.Pp
The user must be named by a
.Cm login user
line and must not have uid 0.
.Pp
system checks the password with
.Xr auth_usercheck 3
and the account with
.Xr auth_approval 3 ,
in a child, at most four at once.
.Pp
After the check, system makes sure again that the client is still
connected and that the user may still log in.
.Pp
A refused login is answered only that it is incorrect.
system logs why.
.Pp
The session's commits record the user's uid, not _nshweb's.
.Pp
The session's terminal is
.Xr nsh 8
on a pty, which system starts as the user, as
.Xr login 1
starts a shell, with
.Fl T
and the routing table the terminal is asked for, if it is asked for
one.
.Sh WATCHING
A session with view may watch commits,
.Cm configure exclusive ,
interfaces, addresses and routes, and config sends it a notice as each
changes.
It may also follow
.Pa /var/log/daemon ,
.Pa /var/log/messages ,
pf's log and, with admin,
.Pa /var/log/authlog .
A log file followed begins with the lines in its last 2 kilobytes, or
its last 64 if the watch asks for more of its past, as
.Xr nshdeck 8
does.
.Sh FAILURE
Nothing is started again.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It A frontend ends, or sends what it may not
Only its session ends.
.It config ends, or sends what it may not
.Nm
takes no more clients, and the parent kills every process but system.
system finishes what it began, within its time limits: a commit, with
its undo or save, a rollback, or the boot.
A commit that still waits for config to lower sessions does not run,
and ends as one undone.
Then
.Nm
ends.
A signal to end
.Nm
still ends it at once.
.It Another process ends, or sends what it may not
.Nm
ends.
.It Nm No ends
Private candidates and sessions are lost.
The kernel's state, the saved configuration, the records and the shared
candidate are kept.
.El
.Sh SECURITY
system does as root what config requests, within the class of the
session config names.
The client's socket tells system which user a session belongs to.
It does not tell system what that user asked for: system trusts config
for that.
So a compromised config can act in the name of any connected client,
but only as that client's login class allows.
A compromised frontend has its own session and nothing more.
A parser holds no privilege, but its answers decide which configuration
runs, and system checks what it answers before using it.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/run/nshd.ro.sockXX" -compact
.It Pa /etc/nshrc
a first configuration, which system saves when there is no saved
configuration
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/nshd
the
.Xr rc.d 8
script, which starts
.Nm
with
.Fl b
.It Pa /var/run/nshd.sock
the read-write socket, mode 0660, group nshadmin
.It Pa /var/run/nshd.ro.sock
the read-only socket, mode 0666
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/nshrc
the saved configuration, root's
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/nshrc.N
copies of the saved configurations, for rollback; rollback 0 is the
newest
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/login
the running login classes and users, and which accounts
.Nm
made
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/commit
the commit record
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/confirm
the record of an unconfirmed commit
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/boot
the record of the boot
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/journal/candidate
the shared candidate, as journal stores it
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nsh 8 ,
.Xr nshdeck 8 ,
.Xr rc.d 8
